model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Runtime readiness admits new work only with a current health lease. Lease expiry or an unhealthy probe preserves dispatched HTTP and WebSocket streams, blocks admission and keeps one bounded renewal watcher running. A fresh healthy probe restores admission. A proven boot change closes the old streams. Membership invalidation and device revocation retain their independent immediate aborts. Health transition logs contain a closed reason, preserved or closed action, bounded remaining lease and probe timing; they never contain response bodies or exception messages. Gateway refusals, relayed runtime refusals, both protection lines and refused runtime commands also carry Home's last reported database pool counters (connections in use, cumulative waits and wait time) with their age. Home upkeep reads them once a minute through Home's capabilities, which costs one indexed single-row query and is bounded at two seconds; a refusal never reads Home, so under a saturated pool the lines carry the last value and its age. The active-health read returns that value without reading Home and adds no lease time; it carries no counters when health itself is refused, and controller-to-endpoint refusals rely on the gateway and upkeep lines."
      constraint "Membership renewal failures preserve only the last acknowledged clean revision until its original expiry plus 24 hours. Failed reads and installs never advance that deadline. Signed invalidation persists a newer dirty revision and aborts that pool before acknowledging a membership writer. Observed account or native-authority changes also persist a veto before fallible runtime repair. An actual runtime membership mismatch persists dirty state and aborts active use before policy installation, including a rejected dirty installation. Pool stream leases register before authority reads and assertion signing, so an in-flight admission cannot miss a concurrent veto. Failed admission releases its lease. A failed first installation, a different revision, dirty state or an elapsed grace deadline never grants model use. Controller reactivation preserves the acknowledged deadline; retained records without an acknowledged grace deadline keep their original lease. Pool views may use this same acknowledged authority while current identity and organization checks remain required."
    }
  }
  extend basalt.poolRuntime {
    metadata {
      constraint "Native policy installation still requires an authenticated signed projection with at most 15 seconds remaining. Only successful clean installation retains the exact account set for at most 24 hours beyond that signed expiry. Rejected installation and expired-envelope replay cannot extend it. A newer dirty policy immediately replaces the clean snapshot and has no grace. Request assertions, candidate-set vetoes and the independent scheduler-plugin health lease remain mandatory. An empty or replaced runtime never invents a previously installed policy. No model request is served that the native-policy plugin would have refused, and a lease never widens what a fused runtime serves: the endpoint cannot tell a readiness refusal caused by a fused plugin from a flip, so readiness is read again right before each container send of a model request, after the container keep-alive, and any readiness refusal, late read or spent lease refuses that send. A readiness read that fails in transport or returns an unparsable body refuses as unhealthy readiness and logs which of the two it was; any other failure stays a defect. With the fleet enabled and no sibling, a restarted primary may take requests before recovery's closing steps finish, because that boot was health-checked and given every current policy before the switch; a dirty policy refuses admission, and a newer clean revision is saved before it is installed but the boot's policy plugin refuses any request assertion whose revision differs from the one it has installed, so no request is served on a boot without the current revision."
    }
  }
}
